# Worked solutions: 5.32 - 5.67 (frequency response, part 2)
S = {
"5.32": ("D", "Minimum-phase systems.", "1. All poles in the left half-plane: true.\n2. Zeros of a minimum-phase system must also be in the left half-plane, not anywhere: false.\n3. For a minimum-phase system the magnitude plot fixes the phase plot uniquely: true.\n\nOnly 1 and 3: **(d)**."),
"5.33": ("A", "Asymptotic Bode plots.", "1. At the corner frequency the actual magnitude of a real zero or pole differs from the asymptote by 3 dB: true.\n2. For a complex pair the phase at $\\omega=\\omega_n$ is $-90^\\circ$ for every $\\xi$ (only the slope of the phase curve depends on $\\xi$): false.\n\nOnly 1: **(a)**."),
"5.34": ("A", "Gain margin at the phase crossover.", "$\\phi=-90^\\circ-\\tan^{-1}\\omega-\\tan^{-1}2\\omega=-180^\\circ\\Rightarrow2\\omega^2=1\\Rightarrow\\omega_{pc}=\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}$.\n$|G|=\\dfrac{2}{\\omega\\sqrt{1+\\omega^2}\\sqrt{1+4\\omega^2}}=\\dfrac{2}{0.707\\times1.225\\times1.732}=\\dfrac43$.\n$GM=\\dfrac1{|G|}=\\dfrac34$ (less than 1, so the closed loop is unstable; Routh on $2s^3+3s^2+s+2$ confirms it): **(a)**."),
"5.35": ("C", "Oscillator: marginal stability from Routh.", "| $s^4$ | 1 | 3 | $K$ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $s^3$ | 2 | 2 | |\n| $s^2$ | 2 | $K$ | |\n| $s^1$ | $2-K$ | | |\n\nSustained oscillation needs $s^1=0\\Rightarrow K=2$.\nAuxiliary equation $2s^2+2=0\\Rightarrow s=\\pm j$, so $\\omega=1$ rad/s: **(c)**."),
"5.36": ("D", "Stable closed loop.", "A stable system needs both a positive gain margin and a positive phase margin: **(d)**."),
"5.37": ("B", "Nyquist stability criterion.", "(A) True: Nyquist uses the open-loop poles (to count RHP poles $P$) and the polar plot (encirclements $N$) to decide $Z=N+P$.\n(R) True: an unstable system has right-half-plane poles. But (R) is a general statement and not the reason the open-loop information suffices.\n\n**(b)**."),
"5.38": ("B", "A time delay adds phase lag only.", "$e^{-s}$ has unit magnitude and phase $-\\omega$ rad. The gain crossover frequency is unchanged, but the extra lag reduces the phase margin by $\\omega_{gc}$ rad. It also moves the phase crossover to a lower frequency, where the gain is higher, so the gain margin falls too.\n\nBoth margins of $G(s)H(s)$ are larger: **(b)**."),
"5.39": (None, "Relative stability.", "1. Phase margin is related to the effective damping: true.\n2. It is the *phase margin* that gives the better estimate of damping ratio, so this is false.\n3. Gain margin in dB is *positive* for a stable system, so this is false.\n\nOnly statement 1 holds, which is not offered. The question as transcribed has an error, so no answer key has been set."),
"5.40": ("C", "Polar plot of a type-2 function.", "$\\angle G=-180^\\circ-\\tan^{-1}\\omega T$, which runs from $-180^\\circ$ at $\\omega=0$ (magnitude $\\to\\infty$) to $-270^\\circ$ at $\\omega=\\infty$ (magnitude $\\to0$).\nSo the plot starts at infinity along the negative real axis, stays in the second quadrant, and ends at the origin tangent to the positive imaginary axis. This is plot (c): **(c)**."),
"5.41": ("B", "Margins near zero.", "A gain margin close to 1 (0 dB) or a phase margin close to 0 means the plot is nearly on $-1+j0$: the system is on the edge of instability and highly oscillatory: **(b)**."),
"5.42": ("A", "Pure delay $e^{-Ts}$.", "$|G(j\\omega)|=K$ for all $\\omega$, so the Nyquist plot is a circle of radius $K$ traced repeatedly.\nIt encloses $-1$ only if $K>1$. Equivalently, $1+Ke^{-Ts}=0$ gives $\\operatorname{Re}s=\\dfrac{\\ln K}{T}$, whose sign depends only on $K$.\n\nStability depends on $K$ only: **(a)**."),
"5.43": ("B", "Gain margin from the Nyquist plot.", "- No crossing of the negative real axis: GM is infinite (4).\n- Crossing between $0$ and $-1$: GM $>0$ dB (3).\n- Passing through $-1$: GM $=0$ dB (2).\n- Enclosing $-1$: GM $<0$ dB (1).\n\nA–4, B–3, C–2, D–1: **(b)**."),
"5.44": ("D", "Gain-phase plot.", "At the phase crossover ($-180^\\circ$) the curve is above the 0 dB line, so the gain there is greater than 1 and $GM<0$ dB. The 0 dB point lies to the left of $-180^\\circ$, so the phase at gain crossover is below $-180^\\circ$ and $PM<0$.\n\nBoth negative (unstable): **(d)**."),
"5.45": ("A", "$GM=1/|G(j\\omega_{pc})|$.", "If the magnitude at phase crossover is $a$, the gain can be multiplied by $1/a$ before reaching the critical point: $GM=\\dfrac1a$: **(a)**."),
"5.46": ("C", "$M_r=\\dfrac{1}{2\\xi\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}}$.", "$M_r=1\\Rightarrow2\\xi\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}=1\\Rightarrow4\\xi^2(1-\\xi^2)=1\\Rightarrow(2\\xi^2-1)^2=0\\Rightarrow\\xi=\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}$: **(c)**."),
"5.47": ("A", "Marginal stability and oscillation frequency.", "$s^3+6s^2+5s+K=0$. The $s^1$ element is $\\dfrac{30-K}6=0\\Rightarrow K=30$.\nAuxiliary equation $6s^2+30=0\\Rightarrow s=\\pm j\\sqrt5$, so $\\omega=\\sqrt5$ rad/s: **(a)**."),
"5.48": ("D", "$PM=180^\\circ+\\angle G(j\\omega_{gc})$.", "$PM=180^\\circ-90^\\circ=90^\\circ$: **(d)**."),
"5.49": ("A", "Plot through $-1+j0$.", "If the plot passes through the critical point, the gain crossover and phase crossover coincide, so $PM=0^\\circ$ and $GM=1$: **(a)**."),
"5.50": ("C", "Evaluate $G(j\\omega)$.", "$G(j1)=\\dfrac1{1+j}$: magnitude $\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2}=0.707$, phase $-\\tan^{-1}1=-45^\\circ$: **(c)**."),
"5.51": ("B", "Corner frequency $=1/T$.", "$\\dfrac1{3s+1}$ has $T=3$, so $\\omega_c=\\dfrac13=0.33$ rad/s: **(b)**."),
"5.52": ("B", "Evaluate $G(j\\omega)$.", "$G(j1)=1+j$: magnitude $\\sqrt2=1.41$, phase $+45^\\circ$: **(b)**."),
"5.53": ("D", "Factorise the denominator.", "$0.66s^2+2.33s+1=(1+2s)(1+0.33s)$.\nCorner frequencies are $\\dfrac12=0.5$ and $\\dfrac1{0.33}\\approx3.0$ rad/s: **(d)**."),
"5.54": ("C", "Negative gain margin in dB.", "A negative gain margin in dB means $|G(j\\omega_{pc})|>1$, so the loop gain exceeds the critical value and the closed loop is unstable: **(c)**."),
"5.55": ("B", "Marginal stability.", "At marginal stability the gain crossover and phase crossover frequencies coincide (the plot passes through $-1$): **(b)**."),
"5.56": ("C", "Nyquist criterion.", "(I) True: it determines closed-loop stability from the open-loop frequency response.\n(II) False as stated: it relates the number of encirclements of $-1$ by the open-loop plot to the difference between closed-loop poles and open-loop poles in the RHP, not the closed-loop poles and zeros.\n\n**(c)**."),
"5.57": ("D", "Overshoot vs resonance peak.", "(I) False: a resonance peak exists only for $\\xi<0.707$, but overshoot occurs for all $\\xi<1$, so systems with $0.707<\\xi<1$ overshoot with no resonance peak.\n(II) True: a large $M_r$ means small $\\xi$ and large overshoot.\n\n**(d)**."),
"5.58": ("C", "Effect of gain on a Bode plot.", "(I) True: gain shifts the magnitude plot only, not the phase plot.\n(II) False: changing the gain moves the gain crossover frequency, where the phase is read, so the phase margin changes.\n\n**(c)**."),
"5.59": ("B", "Gain margin at phase crossover.", "$\\phi=-3\\tan^{-1}(\\omega/5)=-180^\\circ\\Rightarrow\\omega_{pc}=5\\tan60^\\circ=8.66$.\n$|G|=\\dfrac{10}{(\\sqrt{25+75})^3}=\\dfrac{10}{1000}=0.01$.\n$GM=20\\log\\dfrac1{0.01}=40$ dB: **(b)**."),
"5.60": ("A", "Asymptotic Bode plot of $\\dfrac1{s(s+1)}$.", "On the asymptotic plot the gain falls at $-20$ dB/decade up to the corner at 1 rad/s, where it crosses 0 dB, so $\\omega_{gc}\\approx1$ rad/s.\n$PM=180^\\circ-90^\\circ-\\tan^{-1}1=45^\\circ$.\n(The exact values are $0.79$ rad/s and $51.8^\\circ$; the asymptotic answer matches option (a).)\n\n**(a)**."),
"5.61": ("D", "Low-frequency slope $=-20N$ dB/decade.", "A type-1 system has one integrator, so the low-frequency asymptote has slope $-20$ dB/decade: **(d)**."),
"5.62": ("C", "Forced response vanishes at a zero of $H$.", "$Y(s)=\\dfrac1s-\\dfrac3{s+1}+\\dfrac3{s+2}=\\dfrac{s^2+2}{s(s+1)(s+2)}$, so $H(s)=\\dfrac{s^2+2}{(s+1)(s+2)}$.\nThe zeros are at $s=\\pm j\\sqrt2$: a sinusoid at $\\sqrt2$ rad/s is blocked: **(c)**."),
"5.63": ("D", "Phase margin vs damping ratio.", "For a second-order loop, $PM\\approx100\\,\\xi$ (degrees), so it is directly proportional to $\\xi$ (1) and zero when $\\xi=0$ (4).\n\n**(d)**."),
"5.65": ("C", "Resonant frequency.", "The magnitude peaks at $\\omega_r=\\omega_n\\sqrt{1-2\\xi^2}$ (for $\\xi<0.707$): **(c)**."),
"5.66": ("C", "Bode magnitude below the corner.", "$\\dfrac{10}{10+s}=\\dfrac1{1+0.1s}$ has its corner at $10$ rad/s. At $0.1$ rad/s the asymptote is flat at 0 dB: **(c)**."),
"5.67": ("A", "Bandwidth and noise.", "A wider bandwidth passes more noise (noise power is proportional to bandwidth), so excessive bandwidth is avoided: **(a)**."),
}
